WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.488
Now turn the worklist into a program
the authorizing official can see.

2
00:00:04.488 --> 00:00:08.707
Every finding that isn't fixed in the
next change window and isn't covered

3
00:00:08.707 --> 00:00:12.787
by an approved exception becomes a
plan of action and milestones item.

4
00:00:12.787 --> 00:00:18.617
SP 800-53 requires one for each
system, updated from assessments

5
00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:22.549
and continuous monitoring, and the
authorizing official approves it.

6
00:00:22.549 --> 00:00:26.609
Each item names the pillar owner,
the control it bears on, the evidence

7
00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:31.025
that will show it closed, and the
re-assessment that will test that evidence.

8
00:00:31.025 --> 00:00:34.753
Behind those items, the program
wraps each tool's unaltered

9
00:00:34.753 --> 00:00:38.100
output with its provenance
and seals it into a bundle.

10
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.478
Because the same inputs produce
a byte-identical bundle,

11
00:00:41.478 --> 00:00:44.928
an assessor can rebuild it
months later and walk back from

12
00:00:44.928 --> 00:00:48.162
a finding to the raw result
without re-running the tool.

13
00:00:48.162 --> 00:00:51.775
That's the difference between an
archived opinion and evidence.

14
00:00:51.775 --> 00:00:56.352
Re-assessment runs on a calendar the
program lead owns, and after each

15
00:00:56.352 --> 00:01:01.250
remediation wave, so a finding is closed
by evidence, not by a ticket status.

16
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:06.713
BOD 25-01's reporting routes set the
floor for ScubaGear at quarterly.

17
00:01:06.713 --> 00:01:10.420
A Zero Trust Assessment score
is compared across runs only

18
00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:13.976
on the same tool version, or
after a review of what changed.

19
00:01:13.976 --> 00:01:18.925
And drift goes to the authorizing
official, not only to security operations.

20
00:01:18.925 --> 00:01:21.805
A control that passed
every interval and failed

21
00:01:21.805 --> 00:01:24.613
the last one is a change
to an authorized system.

22
00:01:24.613 --> 00:01:28.088
The book calls treating that
drift as something the official

23
00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:31.087
sees the cheapest adoption
step in its final chapter.

24
00:01:31.087 --> 00:01:34.141
So the decision sits with
the authorizing official:

25
00:01:34.141 --> 00:01:36.840
receive interval-to-interval
drift findings,

26
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.035
prepared by the program lead
and security operations,

27
00:01:40.035 --> 00:01:44.438
every assessment interval, and decide
which ones are significant changes.

28
00:01:44.438 --> 00:01:45.910
Stand it up this quarter.
